WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.873
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level three.

2
00:00:06.173 --> 00:00:09.199
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.499 --> 00:00:16.447
Unit six: International Relations Basics. Today you will speculate about past events with modal perfects.

4
00:00:16.747 --> 00:00:19.562
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.628
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.483 --> 00:00:25.542
Diplomacy.

7
00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:30.233
Diplomacy means the skill of managing relations between countries.

8
00:00:30.817 --> 00:00:33.025
Quiet diplomacy ended the crisis.

9
00:00:33.617 --> 00:00:36.710
Your turn. Say: Diplomacy.

10
00:00:39.567 --> 00:00:40.754
Diplomacy.

11
00:00:41.517 --> 00:00:42.332
Treaty.

12
00:00:43.317 --> 00:00:46.431
Treaty means a formal written agreement between countries.

13
00:00:47.017 --> 00:00:49.016
The two nations signed a peace treaty.

14
00:00:49.617 --> 00:00:51.863
Your turn. Say: Treaty.

15
00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:55.376
Treaty.

16
00:00:56.117 --> 00:00:57.338
Alliance.

17
00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:01.859
Alliance means a group of countries that agree to support each other.

18
00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:04.833
The alliance promised mutual defence.

19
00:01:05.417 --> 00:01:08.011
Your turn. Say: Alliance.

20
00:01:10.867 --> 00:01:11.914
Alliance.

21
00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:13.895
Foreign policy.

22
00:01:14.883 --> 00:01:18.729
Foreign policy means a country's plan for dealing with other countries.

23
00:01:19.317 --> 00:01:22.315
Its foreign policy favours trade over conflict.

24
00:01:22.917 --> 00:01:25.662
Your turn. Say: Foreign policy.

25
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:29.745
Foreign policy.

26
00:01:30.483 --> 00:01:31.705
Sanctions.

27
00:01:32.717 --> 00:01:36.783
Sanctions means official penalties used to pressure a country to change.

28
00:01:37.383 --> 00:01:40.021
Sanctions limited the country's oil sales.

29
00:01:40.617 --> 00:01:43.327
Your turn. Say: Sanctions.

30
00:01:46.167 --> 00:01:47.237
Sanctions.

31
00:01:47.983 --> 00:01:48.961
Embassy.

32
00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:54.306
Embassy means the offices of a country's representatives in a foreign capital.

33
00:01:54.917 --> 00:01:57.160
The embassy helped stranded citizens.

34
00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.472
Your turn. Say: Embassy.

35
00:02:03.333 --> 00:02:04.381
Embassy.

36
00:02:05.117 --> 00:02:06.350
Negotiation.

37
00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:10.789
Negotiation means formal discussion to reach an agreement.

38
00:02:11.383 --> 00:02:13.789
The negotiation lasted three weeks.

39
00:02:14.383 --> 00:02:17.140
Your turn. Say: Negotiation.

40
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:21.245
Negotiation.

41
00:02:21.983 --> 00:02:23.101
Sovereignty.

42
00:02:24.117 --> 00:02:27.347
Sovereignty means the power of a country to govern itself.

43
00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:30.658
The treaty respected each nation's sovereignty.

44
00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:33.833
Your turn. Say: Sovereignty.

45
00:02:36.667 --> 00:02:37.737
Sovereignty.

46
00:02:38.533 --> 00:02:42.173
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:43.017 --> 00:02:48.351
Visal, an analyst writes 'Talks could have prevented the conflict.' What does 'could have prevented' tell us?

48
00:02:49.450 --> 00:03:02.784
It speculates about the past: something was possible, but did not happen. We use modal + have + past participle: could have, might have, should have, must have, can't have.

49
00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:05.395
How do the meanings differ?

50
00:03:06.483 --> 00:03:19.645
Should have criticises: the embassy should have warned us. Might have is weak possibility: sanctions might have worked. Must have is a strong conclusion: the treaty must have failed because of mistrust.

51
00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:21.902
Is there a negative?

52
00:03:23.017 --> 00:03:31.453
Yes. The alliance needn't have collapsed means it was unnecessary. They can't have agreed means we are sure they did not.

53
00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:33.984
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:39.276
Grammar focus: Modal perfects: could have and should have and must have.

55
00:03:39.576 --> 00:03:40.740
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:44.262
Early diplomacy could have prevented the conflict.

57
00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:47.637
The embassy should have warned its citizens sooner.

58
00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:51.049
The treaty must have failed because of mistrust.

59
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:59.735
Modal + have + past participle talks about the past: could / might have (possible), should have (criticism), must have (sure), can't have (sure not).

60
00:04:00.483 --> 00:04:03.809
Be careful! Don't say: He must have forgot.

61
00:04:05.767 --> 00:04:08.010
Say: He must have forgotten.

62
00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:10.704
Use the past participle after have.

63
00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:14.094
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:16.586
When Diplomacy Fails.

65
00:04:16.886 --> 00:04:20.198
Historians often ask what leaders could have done differently.

66
00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:26.601
Skilful negotiation might have saved the treaty, and a stronger alliance should have discouraged the attack.

67
00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:33.686
Sanctions may have hurt the economy, but they cannot have changed a foreign policy that leaders refused to discuss.

68
00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:37.965
The embassy must have sent warnings, yet no one acted in time.

69
00:04:38.967 --> 00:04:42.777
Looking back reminds us that patient diplomacy is cheaper than war.

70
00:04:43.867 --> 00:04:48.019
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:48.883 --> 00:04:51.672
Early talks blank the war, but nobody tried.

72
00:04:51.972 --> 00:04:56.032
A, could have prevented. B, could prevent. C, can have prevent.

73
00:04:59.567 --> 00:05:02.089
The answer is A: could have prevented.

74
00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:06.225
Criticism about a past duty uses blank have.

75
00:05:06.525 --> 00:05:09.493
A, might. B, should. C, can.

76
00:05:13.033 --> 00:05:14.708
The answer is B: should.

77
00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:18.593
A formal agreement between countries is a blank.

78
00:05:18.893 --> 00:05:22.418
A, sanction. B, embassy. C, treaty.

79
00:05:25.967 --> 00:05:28.326
The answer is C: treaty.

80
00:05:29.483 --> 00:05:31.227
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:31.527 --> 00:05:37.329
A, He must have forgot.. B, He must have forgotten.. C, He must forgot..

82
00:05:40.867 --> 00:05:43.284
The answer is B: He must have forgotten..

83
00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:47.912
According to the reading, patient diplomacy is cheaper than blank.

84
00:05:48.212 --> 00:05:51.518
A, war. B, trade. C, talks.

85
00:05:55.067 --> 00:05:56.555
The answer is A: war.

86
00:05:57.767 --> 00:06:01.278
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:02.117 --> 00:06:06.380
Why is diplomacy important for resolving conflicts between countries?

88
00:06:15.183 --> 00:06:16.672
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:16.972 --> 00:06:45.578
Diplomacy gives countries a way to solve disagreements through talks instead of force. Through negotiation, embassies and treaties, governments can understand each other's concerns, find compromises and avoid costly wars. It also builds trust over time, so that future crises are easier to manage. Even when relations are tense, open channels reduce the risk of dangerous misunderstandings.

90
00:06:45.878 --> 00:06:47.506
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:48.417 --> 00:06:54.610
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and modal perfects: could have and should have and must have.

92
00:06:54.910 --> 00:07:01.011
Today's value is diplomacy. Disagree respectfully, and look for solutions everyone can accept.

93
00:07:01.311 --> 00:07:04.044
See you in Unit seven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:04.344 --> 00:07:07.577
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

